r#  67819  1267  43
p: .
t. 
|  A second difficulty for push/transfer mechanics is deriving the correct
|  sequence for presenting symbols to the <!!"!!>interpreter<!!"!!> (in real life, said
|  interpreter is more like an abstract representation guiding the compilation
|  of high-level code, but we can still reason about source
.  code as if it were interpreted on-the-fly)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 382
r#  67820  1267  44


--- Sentence//end
id: 382
r#  67962  1269  39
p: .
t. 
|   Usually this sequence is
|  different from the order in which tokens appear in source code, so symbols
.  have to be reordered for push/transfer


--- Sentence/switch
id: 383
r#  67963  1269  40


--- Sentence//end
id: 383
r#  68195  1272  41
p: .
t. 
|   Compilers must also deduce which tokens
|  are associated with which function-symbols, a process that involves
|  both syntax (e.g., nested parentheses) and semantics (e.g., order of operands,
.  like multiplication preempting addition)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 384
r#  68196  1272  42


--- Sentence//end
id: 384
r#  68541  1276  83
p: .
t. 
|   Parsing high-level code is therefore a
|  matter of reducing source files (which have extra structures such as nested
|  expressions) to a simpler representation based on token-sequences
|  (details like nesting become <!!"!!>flattened<!!"!!> to a single lineup, which works
.  because subroutines enforce a contract about how the program stack should be used)

--- Sentence/end 
id: 384
r#  68542  1276  84

--- Paragraph/end 
id: 71
y: N/A


--- Paragraph/start
id: 72
r#  68545  1278  1


--- Sentence/start
