r#  69396  1292  19
p: .
t. 
|  Both computer code and natural language are, by many lights,
|  better represented in terms of parse-graphs than trees
|  (particularly in so far as some relations, like anaphora in the latter context
|  and side-effect propagation in the former, cut across tree-like
.  directed branches)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 389
r#  69397  1292  20


--- Sentence//end
id: 389
r#  69510  1293  64
p: .
t. 
|   With proper edge-filtering, tree <!!"!!>visiting<!!"!!>
.  algorithms (e.g., push/transfer) can be adopted to parse graphs


--- Sentence/switch
id: 390
r#  69511  1293  65


--- Sentence//end
id: 390
r#  69643  1296  21
p: .
t. 
|  Type-checking may be described as a graph-traversal
|  strategy where the <!!"!!>program stack<!!"!!> holds types rather
.  than concrete values


--- Sentence/switch
id: 391
r#  69644  1296  22


--- Sentence//end
id: 391
r#  69947  1302  9
g. 
|   @l(-%>>) +69669/1297/14
|   +69691/1297/36:16=69707/1297/52
|   +69696/1297/41:0=69696/1297/41
.   @l() -69696/1297/41
p: .
t. 
|   Assuming
|  polymorphism, a type-checker
|  must confirm that, given any stack-state with a
|  procedure symbol on top, there is a unique implementation
|  whose parameter-types match those symbolized lower on the stack
|  (if so, its output type is then placed on the stack
.  instead)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 392
r#  69948  1302  10


--- Sentence//end
id: 392
r#  70414  1310  67
p: .
t. 
|   Both type-checking
|  and push/transfer may thereby be modeled
|  as versions of a generic graph-traversal pattern, whose
|  central operations are crossing to unvisited child nodes;
|  pushing leaf-nodes' values (or types, etc.) onto an
|  external stack; and performing some external operation
|  on non-leaf nodes once all descendent branches have been explored
|  (in effect, navigating graphs with a state
.  machine holding stack-memory and a trace of previously-seen nodes)

--- Sentence/end 
id: 392
r#  70415  1310  68

--- Paragraph/end 
id: 73
